        "summary": "The concrete setup list — license, MIBOR and BLC access, tools, your announcement, and a schedule you build before the days fill themselves.",
        "video": "",
        "body": "<p>Your first thirty days will either be structured or they'll be a blur. There's no third option. Nothing on this list is difficult; the difficulty is that there are a lot of small items and none of them will chase you down. Work the list.</p><h3>Week one: get legal and get access</h3><p>Start with the plumbing, because nothing else works until it's done.</p><ul><li><strong>Activate your license with YRL.</strong> Confirm with your broker that the state has you associated with the brokerage. Until that's processed, you cannot practice. Get confirmation in writing, not a verbal yes.</li><li><strong>Join MIBOR and get your BLC access.</strong> In central Indiana, the MLS is the Broker Listing Cooperative, and MIBOR is the association behind it. Membership, dues, orientation, and your BLC login all get handled here. Ask your broker who walks you through it and what the timeline is.</li><li><strong>Get your lockbox credentials set up.</strong> You can't show a house you can't get into. Ask what YRL uses and what it takes to be issued access.</li><li><strong>Take the BLC training.</strong> Not the fifteen-minute version. Learn how to run a search, how to save a search for a client so new listings notify them automatically, and how to pull recent solds. This system is how you'll answer nine out of ten client questions.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><p>Block thirty minutes a day, every day, for your first two weeks purely on learning the BLC. It feels slow. It is the single highest-return thing you can do in month one, because it's how you produce value clients can see.</p></div><h3>Week one: set up your business accounts</h3><p>You're a business now, so separate it from your personal life on day one instead of untangling it in April.</p><ul><li>Open a separate business checking account. Every commission in, every expense out, nothing else.</li><li>Start a folder — digital is fine — for receipts. Dues, signage, mileage, education, marketing.</li><li>Set aside a percentage of every commission for taxes from the very first check. Nobody is withholding for you. Ask an accountant what percentage fits your situation.</li><li>Set up your professional email and voicemail. Your voicemail greeting should say your name, that you're a Realtor with Your Realty Link, and when you'll call back.</li><li>Ask your broker what YRL provides for a CRM, a signature tool, and marketing templates before you go buy anything. Check the Marketing and Vendors pages first.</li></ul><h3>Week two: your database is the business</h3><p>Whatever system you end up using, the first job is the same: get every person you know into one place. Phone contacts, email contacts, holiday card list, old coworkers, your gym, your church, your kid's team parents, the guy who cut your hair for six years.</p><p>Aim for at least a hundred names. Most people who think they know twenty people discover they know two hundred once they actually go looking. For each one, capture name, phone, email, and one line of context — how you know them, where they live, anything you remember about their housing situation.</p><p>This list is your entire year-one business. Not a lead source. The business.</p><h3>Week two: tell everyone</h3><p>Here's where a lot of new agents quietly stall. Announcing feels like bragging, or like asking for something. Reframe it: you're not asking for a favor, you're telling people what you do now, the same way you would if you'd become a nurse. Most of them will be genuinely happy for you.</p><p>Do it three ways. Personally by phone or text to your closest fifty. Publicly on social media. And in writing — email or a mailed card — to everyone else.</p><p>The call is short and the tone is warm, not salesy.</p><div class=\"script\"><p><strong>You:</strong> Hey Dana, it's Marcus. I've got news — I got my real estate license and I've joined Your Realty Link here in Indy. I'm calling the people I actually know before I do anything else, because I'd rather build this on people I like.</p><p><strong>Them:</strong> That's great! Congratulations.</p><p><strong>You:</strong> Thanks. I'm not calling to sell you a house — I know you just moved. But two things. First, if a question ever comes up about the market or what your place is worth, I want to be the person you text. Second, if you hear anybody talking about buying or selling, would you think of me?</p><p><strong>Them:</strong> Yeah, of course.</p><p><strong>You:</strong> I appreciate it. I'll check in every so often, and if I ever get annoying, tell me and I'll cut it out.</p></div><p>Note what that script does. It gives permission to say no, it doesn't ask for a transaction, and it asks for one specific, easy thing: think of me. That's a request people can actually fulfill.</p><h3>Weeks three and four: build the week before it builds you</h3><p>You now control your own calendar, which sounds like freedom and functions like quicksand. If you don't decide what your days look like, other people's urgencies will decide for you.</p><p>Pick a start time and hold it. Not a range. A time. Then build a default week that looks roughly like this:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First 90 minutes, every workday</td><td>Lead generation. Calls, texts, follow-up. Nothing else. Phone off email.</td></tr><tr><td>Mid-morning</td><td>Follow-up on yesterday's conversations, appointments, showings</td></tr><tr><td>30 minutes daily</td><td>Skill work — BLC practice, contract practice, script rehearsal</td></tr><tr><td>Afternoon</td><td>Client work, previewing property, admin</td></tr><tr><td>Last 20 minutes of the day</td><td>Plan tomorrow on paper, in priority order</td></tr><tr><td>One evening or weekend block</td><td>Off. Actually off. Put it on the calendar in ink.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>That last row matters more than it looks. The agents who burn out in year one are usually the ones who were available every hour and still didn't get much done, because unbounded time doesn't get used well.</p><div class=\"callout\"><p>Plan tomorrow before you leave today. Ten minutes of planning tonight buys back an hour of drift tomorrow morning, and it means you start the day executing instead of deciding.</p></div><h3>Also in month one: learn the paperwork cold</h3><p>Find the listing agreement, the buyer agency agreement, the purchase agreement, and the disclosures on the Contracts page. Print one of each. Read every line, and write down every box you don't understand. Then ask your broker to walk you through the list.</p><p>After that, practice. Pick a real listing off the BLC, invent a buyer with a price and a closing date, and write the offer. Have someone experienced check it. Do it again with a different wrinkle — a repair credit, a late possession, personal property staying. Do this a few times a week and by the time a real client is sitting across from you, your hands will know the form and your attention can stay on the person.</p>",
