        "id": "m04l2",
        "title": "Knowing Your Market Cold",
        "minutes": 14,
        "summary": "The study habit that makes you genuinely useful in months instead of years — and lets you answer market questions without looking anything up.",
        "video": "",
        "body": "<h3>Knowledge is the one form of experience you can get in a hurry</h3><p>You can't manufacture twenty closings. You can absolutely manufacture the market knowledge that a twenty-closing agent has, and you can do it in a matter of months, because most of it isn't earned through deals — it's earned through paying attention. Most agents don't pay attention. That's your opening.</p><p>The goal for this month is specific and testable: <strong>you should be able to answer any reasonable market question about your area, out loud, without opening your laptop.</strong> Not perfectly. Not to the dollar. But close enough that the person across from you thinks, this person actually knows what they're talking about.</p><h3>Step one: pick a small area and refuse to widen it</h3><p>New agents make the same mistake — they claim the whole metro. You cannot know the whole metro. You can know one township, two or three ZIP codes, or a set of neighborhoods that share a school district, and you can know them so well that nobody can rattle you.</p><p>Draw your boundary on an actual map. It should be small enough that a year's worth of sales is a number you can hold in your head, but big enough to support a business — a rough target is an area doing somewhere in the range of a few hundred sales a year, which you can check in the BLC before you commit. Write the boundary down. Say it out loud when people ask what you do. Don't move it for at least a year.</p><h3>Step two: build the weekly numbers habit</h3><p>Set a standing appointment with yourself — an hour, same day, same time, every week. Nothing gets to take that slot. In that hour you pull the same searches from the BLC and write the answers down in the same place, so that over a few months you're watching a line move rather than staring at a snapshot.</p><p>Track these for your area:</p><ul><li><strong>Active listings right now.</strong> Total supply.</li><li><strong>Pendings this month.</strong> Your best read on live demand.</li><li><strong>Closed sales this month, and the same month last year.</strong> Direction matters more than the raw number.</li><li><strong>Median sale price and median price per square foot.</strong></li><li><strong>Median days on market.</strong></li><li><strong>Sale price to list price ratio.</strong> Are homes going over ask, at ask, or under?</li><li><strong>Price reductions this month</strong> as a share of active inventory.</li><li><strong>Expired, withdrawn, and canceled listings.</strong> A rising number here is one of the earliest signals that pricing has gotten ahead of buyers.</li></ul><p>Then do the one calculation that turns data into advice. Divide active listings by the number of homes selling per month. That's your months of supply — how long it would take to sell everything currently listed if nothing new came on. Somewhere around six months is the traditional dividing line between a market that favors sellers and one that favors buyers. Below that, sellers hold the leverage. Well above it, buyers do.</p><div class=\"callout\"><p>One month of movement is noise, not a trend. Don't announce a market shift because a single month wobbled. You need roughly three months moving the same direction before you say anything with the word trend in it — and clients will remember if you called it early and wrong.</p></div><h3>Step three: cut your area by price band</h3><p>Here's where most agents stop and where you should keep going. A single set of numbers for your whole area is nearly useless, because the entry-level market and the upper-end market in the same ZIP can be doing completely opposite things. Entry-level might be selling in a weekend with multiple offers while the top band sits for four months.</p><p>Split your area into about five bands — entry, low-middle, middle, upper-middle, and upper — using price breaks that reflect what's actually selling there, not round numbers you picked out of the air. Run months of supply for each. Now, when a seller tells you their price, you can tell them what their specific slice of the market is doing rather than reciting a headline that may not apply to them at all.</p><div class=\"script\"><p><strong>Seller:</strong> I keep reading that the market is slowing down. Should we even be listing right now?</p><p><strong>You:</strong> Depends entirely on where you sit. Overall in this township, yes — inventory is up from where it was in the spring. But your house lands in a price band where there are only about eleven homes competing and roughly six selling a month. That's under two months of supply. The slowdown you're reading about is real, and it's mostly happening two price bands above you. Your buyer pool hasn't thinned much at all.</p><p><strong>Seller:</strong> Nobody's explained it that way before.</p><p><strong>You:</strong> That's the only way it's worth explaining. The headline number covers a lot of very different markets.</p></div><h3>Step four: preview homes until neighborhoods are in your body, not your notes</h3><p>Numbers alone make you sound like a report. Previewing makes you sound like a local. Commit to seeing a set number of homes a week in your area — ten is a serious number and very achievable if you batch them, and it's the single fastest way to build instincts.</p><p>Go to broker opens. Go to public open houses on Sunday. Ask your office for showings you can tag along on. Walk through, and before you leave, guess the price. Write your guess down. Then look it up. You'll be embarrassed for a few weeks and then you'll start landing within a few percent — that's your price-per-square-foot instinct forming, and it's not something you can read your way into.</p><p>While you're there, log the things that don't show up in the data: which streets back up to the interstate, which subdivision has the HOA that runs hot, where the good elementary boundary actually falls, which builder used the finishes that are aging badly, which pocket floods. That's the knowledge clients can't get from an app, and it's why they'll keep you.</p><h3>Then use it out loud</h3><p>Knowledge you never say doesn't count. Once a month, turn your numbers into something short — a plain post, a one-page email to your sphere, a paragraph you text to a past contact. No hype, no cheerleading. Just what happened and what it means for someone buying or selling here. Do that for six months and people will start forwarding it, which is how a new agent quietly becomes the person people ask.</p>",
        "actions": [
          "Draw your farm area boundary on a map and confirm in the BLC that it produced enough sales in the last twelve months to support a business.",
          "Block one recurring hour each week for market study and put it on your calendar as an unbreakable appointment.",
          "Pull the last 90 days of BLC solds in your area and record median price, median price per square foot, median days on market, and months of supply.",
          "Split your area into five price bands and calculate months of supply for each one separately.",
          "Preview ten homes this week, guessing the price before you look it up each time, and log what you were off by."
        ],
