        "body": "<h3>From reacting to deciding</h3><p>There's a moment in this career when the fog lifts. You stop wondering whether business will show up and start deciding how much you'll produce, because you know your ratios and you know what it costs to feed them. That moment doesn't arrive by seniority. It arrives the first time you plan the year on paper.</p><p>Last lesson gave you the raw material. This one turns it into a plan.</p><h3>Start with the number you need, then work backward</h3><p>Don't start with a goal that sounds impressive. Start with your household. Add up what your life actually costs for twelve months — housing, food, insurance, transportation, childcare, debt payments, and something for savings. That's your personal need.</p><p>Then add your business costs, which you'll budget in a minute. Then add taxes. You're an independent contractor at YRL, so nothing is withheld for you — set money aside from every check and talk to a CPA about quarterly estimates if you haven't already.</p><p>Now you have a gross commission income target that means something. Divide it by your average commission per closing from last year, and you have the number of transactions year two requires. Divide by your ratios and you have the number of appointments, and the number of conversations, and finally the number of conversations per working day. That last number is the plan. Everything above it is arithmetic.</p><p>Worked example. Say your life plus business plus taxes requires a gross of $96,000. Your average closing paid you $5,300 last year. That's about 18 closings. If you signed 20 clients and closed 11, you'll need roughly 33 signed clients. At four appointments per signature, that's 132 appointments, and at 13 conversations per appointment, roughly 1,700 conversations — call it 8 a day across a normal working year.</p><div class=\"callout\"><p>If the daily number that falls out is absurd — 40 conversations a day, every day — the plan isn't the problem. The ratios are. Either improve conversion or raise your average commission, because you can't out-hustle bad math forever.</p></div><h3>Build an actual budget</h3><p>Most agents have no budget, just a vague sense that marketing costs money. Write it down instead, in three buckets:</p><ul><li><strong>Fixed cost of doing business.</strong> MIBOR and association dues, BLC access, your license and continuing education, E&amp;O if applicable, whatever YRL charges you — ask your broker for the full list so nothing surprises you in March. Phone, computer, car.</li><li><strong>Lead generation and marketing.</strong> Anything meant to produce a client. This is the bucket to hold to a standard.</li><li><strong>Growth investments.</strong> Training, coaching, a designation, eventually help.</li></ul><p>For the marketing bucket, set a rule before you spend: every recurring expense has to return several times its cost in commission, not one deal's worth. The trap is spending roughly one commission to generate roughly one commission and calling it a success. That's not a business, that's a treadmill with extra steps. Track spend against the source column in your closing spreadsheet, and kill anything that hasn't cleared the bar after a fair trial.</p><h3>Decide what to double down on</h3><p>Your review named the two or three sources that produced most of your income. The instinct in year two is to add five new lead sources. Resist it. The better move is to go deeper on what already works before you go wider.</p><p>If open houses produced, don't do more of them casually — do them systematically, with a real follow-up sequence and a better sign-in conversation. If your sphere produced, expand the list and increase the touch frequency. If one neighborhood produced two closings, own that neighborhood.</p><p>Add at most one genuinely new channel in year two, and give it a full twelve months and an honest measurement before you judge it. Agents who swap strategies every quarter never find out whether any of them work.</p><h3>Should you hire help or join a team?</h3><p>The honest test isn't how busy you feel. It's this: <strong>are you regularly spending hours on work that could be done by someone earning far less than your hourly production rate, and is that work crowding out client contact?</strong> If yes, and if your income is stable enough to cover the cost through a slow quarter, it's time to consider help.</p><p>Most agents' first hire is administrative, not sales — someone to handle files, coordination, and scheduling, so you keep the two things nobody can do for you: talking to people and negotiating. Talk to your broker before you hire anyone, because there are compliance and licensing lines around what unlicensed help can and can't do. The Compliance page is a starting point, but have the conversation.</p><p>Joining a team is a different trade. You typically give up a slice of commission in exchange for leads, structure, accountability, and someone senior in the room when a deal gets hard. That can be an excellent deal for a second-year agent who converts well but generates poorly, and a bad deal for one who generates plenty and just needs systems. Be clear-eyed about which one you are.</p><h3>Keep learning, but pick the right kind</h3><p>Continuing education is required. Real education is a choice. Three categories deserve your money, roughly in this order: <strong>sales skill</strong> (how you handle objections, present, and ask), <strong>business skill</strong> (numbers, cost per client, time control), and <strong>market knowledge</strong> (contracts, financing, property systems, what's actually being built and sold around Indianapolis).</p><p>New agents overspend on the flashy categories and underspend on the one that pays — knowing what to say and how to say it. MIBOR classes, YRL's Agent Training page, designations, and coaching all count. What doesn't count is collecting content you never apply. You aren't paid for what you know. You're paid for what you do with it.</p><h3>Growing income versus growing a business</h3><p>Here's the distinction that shapes the next five years. Growing your income means doing more transactions yourself — more hours, more nights, more weekends. There's a ceiling, and it's usually your calendar and your family's patience.</p><p>Growing a business means building something that produces predictable results because of its systems, not because of your stamina: a database that generates repeat clients, a lead source you understand well enough to turn up or down, documented processes, and eventually people. Income you earn. A business you build.</p><p>Year two is early, and there's nothing wrong with a year that's mostly hustle. Just start writing down how you do things while you're doing them. That's the first brick.</p>",
